From 766adc30e8f1ec2e09452a5aa6bc9bfb1d9b866c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:17:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Glass lighter in 4K HDR on small TVs; HDR10+ kept from the
 decoder when the TV lacks it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Fix 4, the Glass look kept and lightened:
- GlassLoad: "heavy video" is a 4K (2000+ lines, or 3000+ across for wide
  3840x1600 films) HDR video (PQ, HLG or Dolby Vision) on a TV under 1.8 GiB
  (or Fire OS low-RAM). Then the glass copies the film at most 2 times a second
  (or the lower setting) and the cut probe looks every 500 ms instead of 100 ms.
- PlayerActivity tells GlassBackdrop and BarWatch on every track or video size
  change; BarWatch looks half as often (2 s / 8 s) with heavy video.
- The Glass refresh row shows e.g. "8 a second · 2 in 4K HDR" while lowered;
  the stored pref and its key are unchanged.
- With no glass on screen for 5 s, GlassBackdrop lets go of its copy buffers,
  the faded-out pictures, off-screen glass's film shaders and the GlassBakes
  cache, so their textures can leave HWUI's GPU cache (which no public API can
  purge, and whose Android 11 budget at a 4K mode never evicts). A paused
  film's blurred picture is kept. Also on onStop.

Fix 6, HDR10+ masked when the TV can't use it:
- Hdr10PlusMask, ported from Media3 main's HevcSeiUtil (Apache-2.0, credited):
  in each Annex-B HEVC access unit, prefix SEI T.35 messages with the HDR10+
  prefix B5 003C 0001 04 get payloadType 254, in place, without allocation;
  emulation-prevention bytes handled; scanning stops at the first VCL NAL, so
  suffix SEI and Dolby Vision RPU NALs are never touched.
- Hdr10PlusGate reads Display.getHdrCapabilities() (API 24+) at each player
  start and on display changes: mask when HDR10+ is not reported (Android 10+),
  or when no HDR at all is reported on Android 7-9; never when unknown.
- PacedVideoRenderer overrides onQueueInputBuffer for HEVC decoders (and Dolby
  Vision on HEVC), skipping encrypted buffers; logs once when it masks.

Tests: Hdr10PlusMaskTest (15), GlassLoadTest (6), BarWatchTest (1).
Build and all 481 unit tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
